{"content":{"blocks":[{"text":"Yes—given that this is unpublished, only you have legacy data, and cledger is performing a backed-up one-time migration, I withdraw the backwards-compatibility objection. A permanent annals compatibility layer would be unnecessary baggage.\n\nYour conclusions are sound, with a few implementation details.\n\n## Revised calls\n\n1. **Legacy envelopes:** migration is the right answer.\n\n   The migration should verify all repositories before considering itself complete:\n\n   - old and new event counts reconcile;\n   - every old conversation/stream remains discoverable;\n   - old notes remain under a separate backup ref;\n   - new writes use `annals/v1`, `stream`, and `ev2-…`;\n   - rerunning the migration is harmless.\n\n   Keeping the originals in a backup notes ref is better than deleting them.\n\n2. **Extension discovery:** comfortable deferring it.\n\n   The practical contract can be:\n\n   > A producer owns the reader for its extension vocabulary. Consumers import that reader.\n\n   If context-graph eventually needs to inspect repositories without installing every producer, discovery can be added later in annals or interpreted at the context-graph layer. We have not foreclosed that path.\n\n3. **Existing multi-hop data:** this is migration debt, not a product-design blocker.\n\n   New continuations created after `source_event_ids` ships can have precise provenance. New users will normally encounter only:\n\n   - precise reconstruction when ancestors are available; or\n   - ancestor-unavailable fallback when they are not.\n\n   The separate “lineage exists but exact event mapping was never recorded” case applies only to pre-feature continuations. Since you appear to have only one current continuation, we can migrate/backfill it or accept a disclosed coarse reconstruction. That does not justify permanently complicating the product.\n\n4. **The Codex model disclosure belongs to turnbridge**, not cledger.\n\n   Cledger already records `producer.model` when the source states it. Turnbridge’s Codex adapter is the component that cannot preserve it and therefore owes the disclosure.\n\n## Branch and document cleanup first\n\nI would not merge `design/policy-split` wholesale. It contains the desired docs, but its source changes predate the annals rename and would revert `stream` back to `conversation`; its lockfile also reflects the older cledger package state.\n\nInstead:\n\n1. Start from `annals-cledger`.\n2. Bring over these files from `design/policy-split`:\n\n   - `docs/LINEAGE_AND_COMPOUNDING.md`\n   - `docs/POLICY_SPLIT.md`\n   - `docs/TODO_HANDOFF.md`\n\n3. Rewrite section A of `TODO_HANDOFF.md` to reflect the final producer-owned-reader decision and point to annals’ relevant extension contract.\n4. Leave cledger/annals-owned design documents in their owning repositories.\n5. Add the requested relocation footer to cross-project handoff/design docs, for example:\n\n   > If this document describes work owned by another project, move the applicable material into that project before implementing it; do not maintain a second authoritative copy here.\n\n   I would not put that on ordinary product docs or format specs—only documents that intentionally discuss cross-project work.\n\n## Implementation sequence\n\nI’d use small, independently reviewable PRs:\n\n### PR 0 — Base and documentation\n\n- Land `annals-cledger` after cledger’s migration completes.\n- Import the three turnbridge design docs without the stale source changes.\n- Rewrite `TODO_HANDOFF.md` section A.\n- Update terminology from cledger-specific envelope concepts where annals is now authoritative.\n- Run the real-ledger smoke test after migration, not just fixtures.\n\nAcceptance check: the picker sees the expected migrated conversations and all tests pass.\n\n### PR 1 — Native annals filters\n\n- Replace the fetch-everything workaround with:\n\n  ```ts\n  readEvents(repo, {\n    ...,\n    kind: [\"conversation_turn\", \"reasoning\"],\n  })\n  ```\n\n- Add a test proving unrelated extension kinds are excluded.\n- Add or retain coverage for both included kinds.\n\n### PR 2 — Provenance correctness\n\n- Read model identity from `producer.model`, not `actor.id`.\n- Read provider from `producer.provider`.\n- Preserve “unset means the source did not state it”; do not infer a provider from model-name syntax.\n- Add compatibility behavior only if migrated events legitimately lack those fields.\n\n### PR 3 — Disclosure cleanup\n\n- Add the missing Codex model-id-drop disclosure.\n- Move human-only information into `plan.notes`:\n\n  - model substitution details;\n  - ancestor-unavailable warnings;\n  - transcript size reporting.\n\n- Keep only information useful to the receiving model in the import notice.\n- Test that notes reach the terminal but do not enter imported model context.\n\nThis closes item 5.\n\n### PR 4 — OpenCode reasoning marker reduction\n\n- Render OpenCode plaintext reasoning as a native reasoning part.\n- Stop inserting `[visible thinking]` on that path.\n- First test whether OpenCode accepts the fabricated reasoning shape reliably.\n- If rejection can leave a partial session, either make fabrication atomic or defer the native form and record that explicitly in `TODO_HANDOFF.md`.\n\nThat was my 3(b) concern: not that the design is dubious, but that a failed multi-file OpenCode import may leave a half-created conversation. A focused PR makes that easy to probe and contain.\n\n### PR 5 — Precise continuation provenance\n\nBefore implementing the transitive walk:\n\n- extend continuation content with ordered source-event identifiers;\n- version the turnbridge continuation payload;\n- record enough target correspondence to distinguish skipped or combined events;\n- update `readLineage` so consumers do not parse payloads themselves.\n\nFor your one historical continuation, either write a one-time backfill or document it as legacy/coarse.\n\n### PR 6 — Multi-hop reconstruction\n\n- Walk `